" :question/math-academy-id "e-4673"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46cd136a-cc20-51e1-ac90-f8d9b787c5b3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544861}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Powers of Negative Fractions Using the Power of Quotient Rule"} :step/id #uuid "fde68042-0dcb-5358-9469-295c9964cb49" :step/math-academy-id 17144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107083 :tutorial/content "To raise a fraction to a power, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power. This is known as the **power of quotient rule for exponents**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3$ by applying the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3} & =(\\frac{2}{5})\\times (\\frac{2}{5})\\times (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 2\\times 2}{5\\times 5\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8abc3ed3-0ba8-5dc1-8bf8-437985614b92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3273 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c9e8e3ef-fed3-5e60-ae33-e12940a91210" :step/math-academy-id 17997 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106632}}] :activity/title "The Power of Quotient Rule for Exponents" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105965}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494040} :activity/id #uuid "7e7b6dc7-4114-5b93-bb27-c0eeaa8b140f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048683 :topic/id #uuid "b4904126-8a71-5d04-a71d-74c10d4620ba" :topic/math-academy-id 1289} :activit